    "b-30-state-and-events-in-the-default-epoch-cyc-41": {
      "id": "b-30-state-and-events-in-the-default-epoch-cyc-41",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "In the default epoch cycle, fixed admission and worker-slot realization are\nseparate. A shrinking pool of admitted-but-unclaimed work is normal when the\nadmitted set is nearly complete.\nFast refresh events record in-epoch "
        },
        {
          "insert": "kg-maintain --no-tool-runners",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " work and\ncoalescing decisions. Full refresh events record boundary knowledge\nmaintenance after report truth is rebuilt. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pool_below_target",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " covers worker\npool pressure and admitted-target exhaustion. The state reader prioritizes\nunhandled "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pool_below_target",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " events ahead of ordinary completion backlog so\ncapacity-preserving replans are not delayed behind many older completion\nevents."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-30-state-and-events-epoch-regression-pause-42": {
      "id": "b-30-state-and-events-epoch-regression-pause-42",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "epoch_regression_pause",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " records that one epoch regressed more report rows\nthan the pause threshold, so the pipeline recorded its checkpoint but withheld\nthe refill. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "epoch_cycle_error",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " records a failed epoch commit or report build.\nBoth carry enough payload for deterministic routing or an operator to decide\nwhether to repair, revert, or resume."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-30-state-and-events-claims-and-checkpoints-43": {
      "id": "b-30-state-and-events-claims-and-checkpoints-43",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Claims And Checkpoints"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-30-state-and-events-a-worker-may-only-edit-p-44": {
      "id": "b-30-state-and-events-a-worker-may-only-edit-p-44",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "A worker may only edit paths covered by its active target claim. Each claim owns\nan explicit write set, and each claim has exactly one worker state. Runner\nvalidation writes checkpoints under that worker state; failed validations are\nkept as evidence, while selectable checkpoints must pass hard gates and improve\nover baseline. When execution ends, the claim closes and the worker state\nrecords whether the lifecycle ended as exact, timeout, error, cancellation, or\nanother runner-owned terminal state."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-30-state-and-events-facts-45": {
      "id": "b-30-state-and-events-facts-45",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Facts"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-30-state-and-events-facts-are-reusable-prov-46": {
      "id": "b-30-state-and-events-facts-are-reusable-prov-46",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Facts are reusable, provenance-tagged board knowledge. They can describe\nsymbols, types, source shapes, resource findings, duplicate relationships,\nnegative hypotheses, or review constraints. Accepted facts can change future\ntarget packets; rejected or uncertain facts stay visible so workers do not\nrepeat low-value guesses."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-30-state-and-events-the-design-taxonomy-name-47": {
      "id": "b-30-state-and-events-the-design-taxonomy-name-47",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The design taxonomy names these fact classes:"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
